<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Macedon</h1><p class="st-meta">808 BCE – 336 BCE (approx.) · capital Aigai; later Pella</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Argead kingdom of northern Greece that Philip II turned into the dominant power of the Greek world, launching the conquests of his son Alexander.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead</a> kings, who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenos">Temenos</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a>, built a kingdom in the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-macedonia">Lower Macedonia</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a>, modern Vergina. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas-i-of-macedon">Perdiccas I</a> as founder in the 7th century BCE; later writers added an earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caranus-of-macedon">Caranus</a>, traditionally dated 808 BCE. The kingdom expanded over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliacmon">Haliacmon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> plains.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Macedon was a monarchy whose king ruled with a warrior aristocracy of Companions. It was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> c. 512 to 479 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> then courted Greek opinion. Archelaus I moved the capital to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pella">Pella</a> c. 400 BCE and hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and horses supported it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a>, from 359 BCE, built a professional army with the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a> pike.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/amphipolis">Amphipolis</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Chaeronea</a> in 338 BCE and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-corinth">League of Corinth</a>. His murder at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> in 336 BCE brought Alexander to the throne, opening the age of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Macedon" width="320" height="257"><figcaption>Image: Map_Macedonia_336_BC-es.svg: Marsyas (French original); Kordas (Spanish translation) derivative work: MinisterForBadT... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Macedonia_336_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>808 BCE – 336 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aigai; later Pella</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander I, Archelaus I, Philip II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Macedonian; Attic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Argead Macedon, Ancient Macedonia, Argead dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-572&amp;focus=kingdom-of-macedon" aria-label="Kingdom of Macedon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5354 785 640 512" role="img